    "description": "The closest Amazon match to a 5 lb tub of plain BodyTech isolate, which we could not find on Amazon. Retail listings put it at about 30 g of protein in a 35 g scoop, roughly 65 servings per 5 lb, with whey isolate and sunflower lecithin as the only ingredients. Nutricost says it is third-party tested but we found no public certification. It was $102.95 on Amazon when we checked, about $1.32 per 25 g.",
    "pros": [
      "About $1.32 per 25 g, cheaper than any BodyTech isolate",
      "Two ingredients, no sweetener",
      "5 lb tub shipped by Amazon"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No banned-substance certification we could confirm",
      "Bigger 35 g scoop, so check the label for your tub"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "If you would buy BodyTech Unflavored for its plain label, this is the cheaper way to get the same thing in bulk.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein / serving": "About 30 g (listing)",
      "Calories": "Check label",
      "Third-party testing": "Brand-stated only",
      "Best for": "Bulk plain isolate"
    },
    "amazonUrl": "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B01HOPJAAE"
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "BodyTech Protein Powder Review",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/bodytech-protein-powder-review/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is BodyTech whey protein isolate good?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, on the label. It is a genuine whey isolate with 25 g of protein in about 110 calories, no concentrate, and an Unflavored version with just two ingredients. The drawbacks are sucralose in the flavored tubs, no banned-substance certification we could find, and a full price of about $2 per 25 g in the small tub. Buy the big tub or on sale."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much protein is in BodyTech Whey Protein Isolate?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The labels we checked list 25 g of protein per scoop in French Vanilla, Rich Chocolate, Unflavored and Cinnamon Cereal, at about 110 calories. Scoop size varies from about 29 g to 33 g by flavor. One Amazon listing for the 3 lb Rich Chocolate tub says 24 g, so check the panel on the exact tub you buy."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Who makes BodyTech protein?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "BodyTech is The Vitamin Shoppe's own brand, along with the BodyTech Elite performance line. It is sold in Vitamin Shoppe stores, on its website and, for the main products, on Amazon. The Vitamin Shoppe does not publicly name the contract manufacturer for each product, which is common for store brands."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is BodyTech third-party tested?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The Vitamin Shoppe says its own brands pass 320 quality checks and that independent labs verify purity and potency. We could not find BodyTech protein in the Informed Choice, Informed Sport or NSF Certified for Sport registries at the time of writing, and found no public lot-level test results. Drug-tested athletes should choose a certified powder such as Dymatize ISO100."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between BodyTech Whey Tech Pro 24 and the isolate?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Whey Tech Pro 24 blends whey isolate with whey concentrate, gives 24 g of protein and about 120 calories per scoop, and adds lactase and Aminogen enzymes with sucralose and acesulfame potassium. The isolate is pure isolate with 25 g of protein and about 110 calories. Whey Tech Pro 24 is cheaper per gram; the isolate is leaner and lower in lactose."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is BodyTech cheaper at Amazon or Vitamin Shoppe?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on the product and the week. The small isolate tubs were the same $44.99 at both when we checked, but Vitamin Shoppe's 15% Auto Delivery and frequent sales made it cheaper there. Whey Tech Pro 24 in 5 lb was cheaper on Amazon, about $1.35 per 25 g against about $1.69. Compare cost per 25 g on the day."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does BodyTech protein have artificial sweeteners?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most flavors do. French Vanilla and Rich Chocolate isolate use sucralose, Cinnamon Cereal adds acesulfame potassium, and Whey Tech Pro 24 uses both sucralose and acesulfame potassium. The Clear Whey Isolate uses sucralose and stevia. BodyTech Whey Protein Isolate Unflavored contains no sweetener at all, only whey isolate and sunflower lecithin."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "bodytech whey protein isolate powder",
      "bodytech isolate whey protein powder",
      "bodytech protein powder",
      "bodytech whey tech pro 24"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Whey Isolate Picks",
    link: "/best-whey-protein-isolate/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">BodyTech Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      The Vitamin Shoppe's house brand makes a real 25 g whey isolate and a cheaper isolate-concentrate blend. Here is what is in each tub, what you pay per 25 g of protein at Amazon and in store, and who should buy something else.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#lineup">The BodyTech Range</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in the Isolate</a>
      <a href="#whey-tech-pro-24">Whey Tech Pro 24</a>
      <a href="#testing">Is BodyTech Tested?</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#taste">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#compare">BodyTech vs Rivals</a>
      <a href="#who">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          BodyTech Whey Protein Isolate is a genuine isolate with 25 g of protein in about 110 calories, and the unflavored tub is about as plain as protein gets. It is not cheap at full price, it relies on sucralose in the flavored versions, and we could not find an Informed Choice, Informed Sport or NSF certification for it. Buy it on sale or by the big tub, or buy Whey Tech Pro 24 if you do not need a pure isolate.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict on BodyTech
          </h2>

          <p>
            BodyTech is The Vitamin Shoppe's own brand, sold in its stores, on its website and, for the core products, on Amazon. The flagship is <strong>BodyTech Whey Protein Isolate</strong>, which lists 25 g of protein per scoop in every flavor we checked, with about 110 calories, 1 g of carbohydrate or less in vanilla and unflavored, and 0 g of added sugar. The protein base is whey protein isolate only. There is no concentrate blended in to cut cost, which is the first thing to check on any powder that calls itself an isolate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our ranking is simple. The <strong>isolate</strong> is a good product at a mediocre full price: $44.99 for a 1.5 lb tub works out to roughly $1.96 to $2.14 per 25 g of protein depending on flavor, which is no cheaper than Dymatize ISO100 in its big tub. <strong>Whey Tech Pro 24</strong>, BodyTech's isolate and concentrate blend, is the better value, at about $1.35 per 25 g in the 5 lb tub we saw on Amazon. The <strong>Elite Clear Whey Isolate</strong> is a format choice for people who want a juice-style drink, not a value pick. Our <a href="/best-vitamin-shoppe-protein-powder/">Vitamin Shoppe protein powder guide</a> ranks both BodyTech tubs against True Athlete and the name brands on the same shelf.
          </p>

          <p>
            The weak spot is testing. The Vitamin Shoppe says its own brands go through hundreds of quality checks and that independent labs verify purity and potency. That is a company claim, not a public certification. We could not find any BodyTech whey in the Informed Choice, Informed Sport or NSF Certified for Sport programs at the time of writing. If you are drug tested, that alone settles it: buy a certified isolate instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            We rank BodyTech on the label, certification status and cost per 25 g of protein. We have not tasted these products, so taste and mixing notes are summarized buyer feedback. To see how BodyTech stacks up against every leading isolate on the same terms, read our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> roundup.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The BodyTech Protein Range, Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            BodyTech's protein range is small and easy to map. There are three powders most buyers will consider: the <strong>Whey Protein Isolate</strong> in a long list of flavors, <strong>Whey Tech Pro 24</strong> in four flavors, and a <strong>Clear Whey Isolate</strong> that mixes with water into a light, fruity drink. Vitamin Shoppe also runs a second label, <strong>BodyTech Elite</strong>, which it pitches as high-potency formulas for hardcore athletes. Most of the Elite line is creatine, pre-workout and amino products rather than protein.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Scoop</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein source</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whey Protein Isolate, French Vanilla</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">110</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">28.9 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whey isolate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whey Protein Isolate, Rich Chocolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">110</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">31.6 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whey isolate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whey Protein Isolate, Unflavored</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">110</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whey isolate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whey Tech Pro 24, Vanilla Ice Cream</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Isolate + concentrate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whey Tech Pro 24, Rich Chocolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">32 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Isolate + concentrate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Clear Whey Isolate (Arctic Frost)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">110</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">33 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whey isolate</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Figures come from the nutrition panels on The Vitamin Shoppe's product pages at the time of writing and vary by flavor. The isolate's flavor list goes well beyond the basics: alongside French Vanilla, Rich Chocolate and Unflavored there are Cookies and Cream, Cinnamon Cereal, Fruity Cereal, Frosted Cereal and Chocolate Peanut Butter, among others. The cereal flavors are where the label changes most, so read the next section before you pick one.
          </p>

          <p>
            What is on Amazon? When we searched, Amazon carried the isolate in French Vanilla, Rich Chocolate, Unflavored, Cookies and Cream and Cinnamon Cereal, mostly in 1.5 lb and 3 lb tubs, plus a 5 lb Cinnamon Cereal tub. Whey Tech Pro 24 was there in 2 lb and 5 lb sizes, and the clear whey was listed as <strong>BodyTech Elite Clear Whey Isolate</strong> in Strawberry Lemonade (26 oz, 23 servings). The 5 lb French Vanilla isolate (78 servings at Vitamin Shoppe), the small 15-17 oz tubs and several specialty flavors did not show up on Amazon, so buy those in store or on Vitamin Shoppe's site.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want a 5 lb tub of plain isolate shipped by Amazon, BodyTech does not offer one there as far as we could see. The closest Amazon equivalent is Nutricost's unflavored 5 lb isolate, which is on the card list below and covered in our <a href="/nutricost-protein-powder-review/">Nutricost protein review</a>. For a non-whey or vegan option, BodyTech is not the brand to shop; our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">plant-based protein guide</a> is the better starting point.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The BodyTech Powders Worth Buying
          </h2>

          <p>
            Three BodyTech powders, isolate first, plus two Amazon alternatives: a certified isolate for tested athletes and a cheaper plain isolate in a 5 lb tub. Label figures are per the Vitamin Shoppe nutrition panels at the time of writing and vary by flavor. Prices are the Amazon prices we saw at the time of writing and change often. We have not tasted these products, so taste notes are buyer feedback.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in BodyTech Whey Protein Isolate
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the simplest tub. <strong>Unflavored</strong> lists exactly two ingredients: whey protein isolate and sunflower lecithin, which helps it mix. Per 30 g scoop it gives 25 g of protein, 110 calories, 0 g of fat, 1 g of carbohydrate, 0 g of sugar and just 45 mg of sodium, per the label at the time of writing. That is 83% protein by weight, with no sweetener, gums or flavoring. It is the BodyTech product we would buy first for baking, oatmeal or blending into a smoothie that already has flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>French Vanilla</strong> adds natural and artificial flavor, sea salt, potassium chloride, sucralose and sunflower lecithin. A 28.9 g scoop gives 25 g of protein, 110 calories, 1 g of fat, 1 g of carbohydrate and 1 g of sugar with none added, plus 115 mg of sodium. That is about 86% protein by weight, which is excellent for a flavored powder and better than many isolates sold at the same price. <strong>Rich Chocolate</strong> adds alkalized cocoa, which pushes the scoop to 31.6 g, carbohydrate to 3 g including 1 g of fiber, and sodium to 170 mg.
          </p>

          <p>
            The cereal flavors are a different recipe. Cinnamon Cereal still lists 25 g of protein and 110 calories in a 31 g scoop, but the ingredient list adds xanthan gum, a sunflower creamer made with sunflower oil, maltodextrin, modified food starch and mono- and diglycerides, and acesulfame potassium alongside sucralose. Chocolate Peanut Butter adds peanut flour, MCTs and a gum blend, and carries a peanut allergen warning. None of that is unsafe, but it is no longer a two-ingredient isolate, so pick vanilla or unflavored if a short label is why you are buying.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SWEETENER CHECK</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Every flavored BodyTech isolate we checked uses sucralose</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">French Vanilla and Rich Chocolate use sucralose alone. Cinnamon Cereal adds acesulfame potassium. Only Unflavored is free of sweeteners. If you avoid artificial sweeteners, buy Unflavored and add your own fruit, cocoa or honey, or see our guide to protein powder without artificial sweeteners.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Because it is an isolate, BodyTech is lower in lactose than regular whey, and Vitamin Shoppe describes the unflavored version as low in lactose. Lower is not zero. Most people with mild lactose intolerance handle a good isolate fine, but anyone with a milk allergy should avoid whey of any kind, and anyone unsure should check with a doctor or registered dietitian. Our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> guide explains what the extra filtering removes and when it is worth paying for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="whey-tech-pro-24" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Whey Tech Pro 24: The Better-Value BodyTech
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>BodyTech Whey Tech Pro 24</strong> is the brand's everyday whey, and the name tells you the protein figure. Per the label at the time of writing, a 30 g scoop of Vanilla Ice Cream gives 24 g of protein, 120 calories, 1 g of fat, 2 g of carbohydrate and 1 g of sugar. Rich Chocolate uses a 32 g scoop for the same 24 g of protein and 120 calories. The protein is a blend of whey protein isolate and whey protein concentrate, so it is not an isolate, whatever the tub's large isolate wording suggests at a glance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two ingredients set it apart from the isolate. It contains <strong>Aminogen</strong>, a protease enzyme blend that brands add to help break down protein, and <strong>lactase</strong>, the enzyme that digests lactose. The lactase is a sensible addition to a product that includes concentrate, though Vitamin Shoppe makes no specific claim about how much it helps. The sweetener system is heavier than the isolate's: sucralose plus acesulfame potassium, with xanthan gum for body. Cholesterol is 25 mg per scoop, a sign of the concentrate.
          </p>

          <p>
            The case for Whey Tech Pro 24 is price. The 5 lb Vanilla Ice Cream tub was $97.38 on Amazon when we checked, for 75 servings, which works out to about $1.35 per 25 g of protein. That is roughly a third cheaper per gram than the isolate in a 1.5 lb tub, and close to the cheapest big-brand wheys. At Vitamin Shoppe the 5 lb Rich Chocolate tub was $114.99 for 71 servings, or about $1.69 per 25 g, so Amazon was the better buy on the day we looked.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whey Tech Pro 24 is BodyTech's answer to Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard: a 24 g isolate and concentrate blend with enzymes, in big tubs. Reviewers have long pointed out that the two labels look very similar. Gold Standard has the advantage of a long track record, while BodyTech often wins on price per 25 g. Our <a href="/optimum-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Optimum Nutrition review</a> covers Gold Standard's label and pricing if you want to compare tubs directly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Who should choose it over the isolate? Anyone who digests regular whey without trouble and wants the most protein per dollar from this brand. Who should not? Anyone who gets bloated from concentrate, anyone avoiding acesulfame potassium, and anyone counting every carbohydrate. For those buyers the isolate, or the unflavored isolate in particular, is worth the extra cost.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is BodyTech Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the question that decides BodyTech for a lot of buyers, so here is exactly what we found. The Vitamin Shoppe's Quality Promise says its proprietary brands go through 320 quality assurance steps and that independent, third-party labs verify ingredient purity and potency. BodyTech product pages describe the products as rigorously tested for quality, purity and potency. Those are company statements, and we have no reason to doubt that testing happens.
          </p>

          <p>
            What we could not find is a public, product-level certification. None of the BodyTech protein pages we checked carried an Informed Choice, Informed Sport, NSF Certified for Sport or BSCG logo, and we found no BodyTech whey in those registries at the time of writing. We also found no lot-level certificates of analysis published for buyers to check, and no independent heavy-metals results for BodyTech protein from Clean Label Project, Labdoor or similar testers.
          </p>

          <p>
            The difference matters. A company sending samples to a lab it chooses is quality control. A certification such as Informed Choice or NSF Certified for Sport means an outside program screens products and batches for banned substances and publishes the result, so anyone can verify it. For most gym-goers, a mainstream retailer's own quality program is probably adequate. For athletes subject to drug testing, it is not, because the burden of proof falls on you if a supplement causes a failed test.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Drug-tested athletes should not use BodyTech protein</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Not because it is known to be contaminated, but because we could not confirm any banned-substance certification. Dymatize ISO100 carries the Informed Choice logo per retailer listings and costs about the same per 25 g in its 5 lb tub. Confirm the certification in the registry for the exact flavor and lot you buy.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you want an isolate with both a certification and published heavy-metals data, our guide to <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> lists the products with public results. Our broader guide to <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a> explains what the different testing programs do and do not check.
          </p>
        </div>

